From faed958a72b125209710d48815d9ea2c5f9c8a08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Jun 2026 14:19:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03277/11362] [outer-loop] kaggle: probe the REAL submission
 generator config (ctx=16384) for the OOM cliff

The competition scored-rerun log is NOT retrievable (Kaggle hides the hidden-data rerun stdout), so
the LLM-tier diagnostic added to the submission kernel prints where nobody can read it. The correct
channel is a standalone probe kernel whose SAVE-RUN output IS retrievable (carnot-arc-binary-smoke).

The prior smoke loaded the generator at -c 4096 (11.4GB used / 4.8GB free on the P100) -- but the
SUBMISSION agent runs n_ctx=16384 (4x the q8 KV), which scaling that footprint blows past 16GB. This
is the suspected OOM cliff that silently degrades the eval agent to CPU graph-explore (-> stuck 0.08).

Updated the probe to test three configs in fresh servers and emit a VERDICT: submission_ctx16384_mtp_on
(the exact frozen config), fallback_ctx16384_mtp_off (the CARNOT_ARC_MTP=0 fix), baseline_ctx4096_mtp_on
(known-good control). Records per-config server_started / vram_after / oom_in_log, so the result is
directly actionable: which config actually loads on the 16GB eval GPU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
